(6366545, 909570, 93565, '<td valign="top">I really like how brooding this is. I''m also guessing that had you exposed properly for the foreground the subject (as it is in the final product) would have been close to blown as well, so this is interesting in that the underexposure shifts our concept of the subject matter. I can''t help but think that if the foreground was exposed for, it would have been about the countryside, a typical pastoral scene. On the contrary, with your approach, we are left with a scene of dreariness, yet hope, the road delving into the future, towards the only light in a world of dark.<br/>Reminds me, unsurprisingly, of the book by Cormac McCarthy <u>The Road</u>. Great shot.</td>', 'I really like how brooding this is. I''m also guessing that had you exposed properly for the foreground the subject (as it is in the final product) would have been close to blown as well, so this is interesting in that the underexposure shifts our concept of the subject matter. I can''t help but think that if the foreground was exposed for, it would have been about the countryside, a typical pastoral scene. On the contrary, with your approach, we are left with a scene of dreariness, yet hope, the road delving into the future, towards the only light in a world of dark.Reminds me, unsurprisingly, of the book by Cormac McCarthy The Road. Great shot.', '2011-03-29 22:19:36.000000', NULL, 0),
